1
00:00:00,556 --> 00:00:02,606
A model for network security.

2
00:00:03,141 --> 00:00:08,711
It shows how a secure channel is built,
layer by layer, from algorithms and keys.

3
00:00:10,556 --> 00:00:13,506
Host A and Host B sit on an open channel.

4
00:00:13,971 --> 00:00:18,481
An attacker may observe, inject,
delete or reorder messages.

5
00:00:19,111 --> 00:00:21,241
Intermediaries sit in between.

6
00:00:22,956 --> 00:00:25,986
The secure transfer
protocol has two parts.

7
00:00:26,591 --> 00:00:28,541
An algorithm for the transformation,

8
00:00:29,191 --> 00:00:30,161
that is encryption.

9
00:00:30,951 --> 00:00:33,961
And a method to distribute
or generate secrets,

10
00:00:34,551 --> 00:00:35,701
that is key management.

11
00:00:37,689 --> 00:00:40,139
The model generalizes to any layer.

12
00:00:40,629 --> 00:00:43,199
Secure email is application layer.

13
00:00:43,729 --> 00:00:45,399
SSL is session layer.

14
00:00:45,939 --> 00:00:47,689
IPsec is network layer.

15
00:00:48,259 --> 00:00:50,129
Same idea, different layer.

16
00:00:51,856 --> 00:00:54,626
A fifth element: trusted third parties.

17
00:00:56,446 --> 00:01:00,136
A certificate authority or a Kerberos
server arbitrates trust disputes.

18
00:01:01,922 --> 00:01:03,552
Algorithms transform.

19
00:01:04,107 --> 00:01:05,497
Keys control them.

20
00:01:05,972 --> 00:01:07,922
Trusted parties arbitrate.

21
00:01:08,407 --> 00:01:11,737
That is the model behind
every secure system.

